training outdoors has a ton of amazing  advantages and in my recent video  discussing how the environment shapes  you an implicit argument was that  training outside and in the wild was  often better than training in a plush  gym or at home because you have that  more chaotic nature but some commenters  politely pointed out that there were  issues with training outside all the  time they discussed how you can't  measure progressive overload in the same  way they talked about maybe how they  don't have access to a nice park and a  lot of people were just asking how do  you program something like this at all  so in this video i thought i'd talk you  through how you can train outdoors and  how you can incorporate this kind of  more wild training into your normal  routine we're gonna train like tarzan  let's go  so first let's talk about why you should  train outside and why being brought up  in the wild like tarzan really could  result in some phenomenal performance  and the simple way to think about this  is that the body craves complexity i'm  talking about chaos i'm talking about  variability if you're constantly  training in the same condition in the  same environments then you're going to  find that you ultimately hit a kind of  plateau so every time you pick up a  barbell in a gym it's the exact same  movement pattern you're lifting a  straight smooth bar off of a straight  flat ground conversely if you pick up a  heavy log for example now it's a grip  challenge so you have to try and grip on  to the bark and prevent it from slipping  out your hand at the same time it  becomes a stability challenge for your  shoulders because the weight isn't going  to be evenly distributed you're going to  find that it rocks more to one side and  that's also going to engage your  obliques and your hips and your  multifidus and when you've finished  pressing a log you can just launch it as  far as you like which is pretty cool and  it builds ballistic strength if you do  that in the gym with a barbell what i  find is you get kicked out and according  to nikolai bernstein this can help to  develop more robust motor patterns that  then translate better to the real world  because when you move something in the  real world it's not going to be barbell  shaped  there's a fine line to be walked here  because something like a push-up on a  ring is so much more likely to lead to a  wrist injury or to crush your fingers  but imagine that once you've built up  that amount of strength and control how  much less likely you are to injure your  wrist in other exercises that require  that amount of risk control  this is just a much more sensory rich  experience this is about not only  exerting strength but exerting strength  in the correct way for your environment  it's about responding to the visual  motor landscape which is what you need  to be able to do if you're in a real  setting you're training your nervous  system to become more agile more stable  and better able to react to what's going  on around it this is also what brings  the brain to life if you repeat the same  movement over and over again it becomes  a rote you don't need to focus however  if you train in a novel an exciting way  something that maybe has a small element  of risk and we do need to be very  careful doing this kind of stuff then  you're going to wake up the brain you're  going to trigger those dopamine systems  and plasticity is going to kick in in a  big way this is just a far more engaging  way to train and by the same token it's  much more fun and exciting  finally this provides the kind of  balance and the kind of complexity that  training program needs to prevent  overlooking certain muscle groups that  could lead to imbalances and later on  issues with movement for example running  on a flat surface can often lead to  issues with it band pain because you're  running in a straight line all the time  you're never dodging from side to side  leading to tightness trail running on  the other hand especially in barefoot or  minimal shoes is going to help you to  develop the kind of stability you need  in the legs and the hips to prevent that  sort of pain and to prevent knee pain  help your knee track more easily as i've  talked about in the past there are too  many small muscle groups too many areas  we can easily overlook if we train with  just a few movement patterns but by  using the kind of variety and complexity  and unpredictability that the  environment provides us with then we can  make sure that we naturally cover all of  these bases and it's no surprise that  the natural environment does all this  because that's the environment that we  evolved in that's the environment that  shaped us so it's not so much the case  that the environment happens to be just  right for us it's more the case that we  happen to be just right for the  environment  so that's enough about why you should go  and train outside and in the wild the  next question is how do you go about  doing it and i think i'll start with the  first and biggest objection i often hear  which is that it doesn't allow for  progressive overload if you're picking  up logs and rocks each time you pick  them up they're going to be a different  weight but for starters this doesn't  need to be your only form of training  that's not what i'm about at all in fact  my whole shtick is that you should  combine different training modalities  that includes this more than that  inspired type stuff alongside other  forms like bodybuilding like  powerlifting so if you're doing this on  the side you can view it just as  accessory work that's going to  strengthen you a little bit more outside  of that strict range of motion and  thereby improve your strength and your  lifts overall so i'm trying to learn the  ring muscle up at the moment i've never  actually had the opportunity to learn it  before because my pull-up bar is too low  but i've recently been really sold on  its merits so i thought it'd be a cool  skill to add to my arsenal  however it's much harder here on the  street branch because it's impossible to  get those rings to be not only the exact  same height but also to stay in the same  position as i'm transitioning through  the movement and that adds that whole  extra challenge of course i probably  would like to take this back to a  straight bar and practice and refine  movement there but then going to a  situation like this is going to help to  make that movement far more  robust once i've got it  so i'm going to say something that  sounds a little bit sacrilegious on  youtube fitness right now which is that  don't worry so much about progressive  overload do you need progressive  overload to improve absolutely making  your training harder is literally the  only way you can get stronger but this  doesn't mean you need to be really anal  tracking your numbers on every single  lift because here's the thing if you go  to the woods and you lift up a pretty  heavy log and the most you can do is 10  repetitions before you need to put it  down because you notice that your form  is starting to suffer  then what you've done there is you've  trained to your maximum rate of  perceived exertion or your maximum safe  rpe and then if you go to the woods and  you do the same thing the next day and  the next day and the next day eventually  when you come back to that initial log  you'll find that you can maybe do 11  reps in other words if you're training  as hard as you can every single time and  if you're just listening to your own  body then you'll naturally get better at  lifting up logs or whatever else it is  if you take a calisthenics approach to  things and if you focus more on being  able to unlock certain skills and moves  then you can use progressions in your  exercises  instead of increasing the amount of  weight so for instance if you want to be  able to do a front lever or a planche  then doing it out in the wild on uneven  terrain it's going to add a little bit  of extra challenge but you'll still  follow the exact same progressive  structure in your training you'll still  go from pseudo-planche push-up to tuck  planches to straddle planches to full  planche  and the variation in the tree branch  might mean that you don't progress quite  as linearly there will be days where you  can't do as many pull-ups as the day  before but this isn't actually the end  of the world you're still going to be  trending upwards and you're also filling  in those little gaps along the way that  you can't necessarily measure we  shouldn't be so fixated on numbers or of  course you can keep going back to the  same spot use the same log the same  branch and then you can literally see  yourself progress and get better at  those things however that's kind of  missing the point next issue that a lot  of you have brought up when it comes to  training outside and in a more wild  environment is that you just don't have  access to something like this and  therefore you can't train this way many  people comment saying like where do you  live man it's beautiful etc etc and  yeah i am very fortunate to have stuff  like this near me however let's be real  it's not like paradise here this is a  really small nature reserve it takes  about five minutes to walk all the way  across it i found a kind of like  clearing in the woods where not too many  people pass through but i'm still  stopping all the time for people with  dogs they're checking out my rings etc  that's not a euphemism basically it's  not the perfect environment to be  training in but i'm just making do and  some people commented on my crawling  video and said that that was a beautiful  spot well that was actually just  literally a big patch of grass in a  housing estate next to a pond that's  always empty and covered in bugs i just  used clever camera angles to make it  look beautiful  i do live in oxfordshire and there are a  lot of really beautiful places around me  i drive to places like brill sometimes  to watlington and  i'm lucky that i've got that option but  there's still a 20 or 30 minute drive  and i make the trip more for the videos  rather than for the training  in the past i've trained literally on a  tree on the side of a road and there's  so much you can do with the tree as i  hope you're seeing so much you can do  with a log but if you don't have access  to even a tree you still don't need to  worry because you can train this way at  home even indoors just by choosing the  right equipment you just want to  simulate this kind of environment so  something like a sandbag is the perfect  example of that because a sandbag isn't  a straight bar that moves in a  predictable manner in fact it's even  less predictable than any other stuff  out here  the weight inside the bag shifts around  it's unaccommodating resistance so every  time you perform a snatch or a clean  it's going to be slightly different and  this is something i've talked about in  the past likewise you can train with  medicine balls you can climb ropes you  can put gymnastics rings on some bars  even at your gym so there's loads of  options even just tie a bed sheet around  a door and do some bodyweight rows you  just want to mix up the exact angle the  exact grip and keep doing new things and  incorporate that kind of training into  your main workouts and even with just  body weight if you want to experiment a  little bit more with crawls and with  various mobility movements a little bit  of movement training and maybe some  calisthenics that you haven't done  before some hand balancing all that  stuff will have the same effects as this  of making your workouts more engaging  for the nervous system waking up your  brain and building those supporting  muscles  if you guys want a training program that  involves a bit more complexity and chaos  and variability to build a more  comprehensive and performant physique  then you can check out my ebook and  training program super functional  training i'll put a link to that in  description down below and there's a  discount on right now whilst we're still  dealing with a pandemic alternatively if  you just want to learn about more  conventional functional training what  that means for the average joe or for an  athlete then you can check out my print  book functional training and beyond and  you get that from all good bookstores  but there'll be a link to that in the  description down below also so for  videos like this one i do a lot of stuff  that's pretty goofy that i think  hopefully looks cool and will translate  well on camera i don't necessarily  recommend that you do all the stuff i'm  doing  in this video because yeah it's a little  bit  out there and it creates the risk of  stuff like this happening  i suffer from my art what can i say but  when i would train like this for real  without the camera rolling what i'd  basically do is come to a spot like this  warm up do some big or more complex  movements at the start essentially that  might be something like a difficult  calisthenics movement like some hand  balancing or some front levers or some  attempts at muscle ups or i might do  some bigger lifts with some logs then i  might move on to some like cool stunts  that i make up on the spot that i think  look exciting or interesting and could  offer some benefits but without pushing  myself too hard i'm already fatigued at  this point so in that example that i  just made up here right now on the spot  i'm using my lats and my biceps to pull  myself towards the tree trunk which is  creating enough friction that i can  climb up it at the same time as a grip  challenge as i'm really gripping onto  the bark and i've got that added  incentive because if i fall there's like  a bed of stinging nettles down there  then at the same time i'm pushing  through my quadriceps and my calves to  drive myself up and of course it's a  balancing task and it's a quadripedal  movement so just made up right there  does loads of stuff and i challenge you  to find an exercise in the gym that  trains quite so many things especially  in a multi-sensory manner and then i'll  just finish with some endurance cardio  usually in the form of some closed chain  calisthenics things like push-ups  pull-ups that can do for a high number  of repetitions to burn some fat at the  end so i'm building strength building  control and balance  experimenting and enjoying myself at the  same time burning some fat and improving  my cardio capacity so that's how i would  structure a workout like this i don't  think you want too much structure by its  very nature you want to be open to  options but if you have a few ideas what  you want to achieve going in then yeah  there's no reason you can't get a  fantastic workout out in the wild  whoever you are wherever you live  i think and i hope you guys agree if you  have any more questions or objections  let me know in the comments below and  i'll do my best to respond to them  perhaps in a future video thank you so  much for watching this one guys i hope  you found it useful and interesting if  you did then please leave a like and  share around that helps me out immensely  you want to see more like this then  subscribe hit the bell button if you  want to be notified of future uploads  and yeah i'll see you on the next one  thanks for watching  and bye for now

